+#!/usr/bin/env bash
+set -o pipefail
+
+seed="${RSPEC_SEED:-4941}"
+TIMEFORMAT='Wall time: %3R seconds (user %3U, sys %3S)'
+time bundle exec rspec spec/models --seed "$seed" --format progress --profile 25
diff --git a/docs/TEST_PERFORMANCE.md b/docs/TEST_PERFORMANCE.md
new file mode 100644
index 0000000..d7a2bc9
--- /dev/null
+++ b/docs/TEST_PERFORMANCE.md
@@ -0,0 +1,70 @@
+# RSpec performance profiling
+
+## Workflow and scope
+
+Profile one spec group at a time; do not run the whole RSpec suite to measure
+these changes. System specs and streaming-tagged specs are excluded from the
+normal test run and should stay out of these measurements. For the current
+phase, use `bin/profile-model-specs` (it runs only `spec/models`). The default
+seed is pinned at `4941` for repeatable ordering; override it with
+`RSPEC_SEED=<number> bin/profile-model-specs` when checking order sensitivity.
+
+The next phase should establish an independent baseline using only
+`spec/requests`; do not infer request performance from the model measurements.
+Factory changes are intentionally deferred to a later phase.
+
+## Models baseline
+
+Measured before the shared setup changes in this task, using seed `4941`:
+
+- **1,795 examples, 0 failures**
+- **156.03 seconds wall time** (RSpec: 2m 33.1s; files loaded in 2.51s)
+- Slow-example profile: the top 25 examples accounted for 30.91s (20.2% of
+  example time).
+
+The slowest recurring groups included `AccountStatusesCleanupPolicy`,
+`Trends::Statuses`, `TagFeed`, `Status::ThreadingConcern`, `MediaAttachment`,
+`Status`, and `PublicFeed`. In particular, the profile showed large trend-query,
+status-threading, and media-processing examples. Keep those measurements in
+mind when the later factory/setup phase begins; this phase deliberately avoids
+refactoring individual examples or factories.
+
+## Shared setup findings
+
+- `rails_helper.rb` forced `Sidekiq.testing!(:inline)` before every example.
+  For model specs this executed queued callbacks/workers even when examples only
+  needed to exercise the enqueueing action. The model group now runs in
+  thread-scoped fake mode by default; the three email assertions that depend on
+  mail delivery jobs are explicitly tagged `sidekiq_inline: true`. Non-model
+  spec types retain their existing inline behavior.
+- The common after-example cleanup enumerated all Redis keys and then deleted
+  them. Model examples now use `FLUSHDB` on the same test Redis database, avoiding
+  the key-list response and separate delete command. Other spec types keep the old
+  cleanup.
+- Model specs do not need the per-example route-reload guard, so it is skipped
+  for `type: :model`. The DNS safety stub remains in place for model examples;
+  it is not disabled to save time because the guard protects against accidental
+  real DNS queries.
+- The test cache remains a `MemoryStore` and is still cleared after every
+  example. Cache cleanup and the DNS/route changes were kept narrowly scoped;
+  avoid broadening their scope without checking test isolation and behavior.
+
+## Models result after shared setup changes
+
+Measured after the changes with seed `4941` (two runs of the final configuration):
+
+- **1,795 examples, 0 failures** on both runs.
+- Wall time ranged from **115.95s** to **125.88s**; the most recent run was
+  **125.88s** (RSpec: 2m 2.7s; files loaded in 2.64s).
+- Against the 156.03s baseline, those runs represent **19.3–25.7% faster**; the
+  mean of the two after-runs is 120.92s (**22.5% faster**).
+- The most recent run's top 25 examples accounted for 22.70s (18.5% of example
+  time).
+
+Timing varies noticeably between runs in this environment. Sidekiq fake mode is
+the principal shared-setup improvement, but these measurements do **not** reach
+the requested 30–35% target. The remaining top groups are largely
+example/data-work dominated (notably trend queries, cleanup-policy queries,
+status threading, feeds, and media processing). Keep this shortfall and the
+profile above in view during the next phase; factories and individual examples
+were intentionally left untouched here.
